## Purpose and audience

Purpose: the concise boolean expectation forms accept real computed conditions
and reject nothing they should accept. Audience: engineers using std.spec.

## Operator workflow

1. Run `bin/simple test test/01_unit/spec/expect_bool_spec.spl`.
2. Every scenario must pass; a failure is a regression in the matcher API.

## Compatibility and limitations

Covers the boolean matcher forms only; chained matchers are out of scope.

## Scenarios

### Concise boolean expectations

#### accepts a runtime-computed true condition

- Verify: accepts a runtime-computed true condition


<details>
<summary>Executable SSpec</summary>

Runnable source: 3 lines folded for reproduction.
Reproduction: this block contains the complete executable scenario source.

```simple
# @req: REQ-SSPEC-LOCAL-001
step("Verify: accepts a runtime-computed true condition")
assert_true("feature".starts_with("feat"))
```

</details>

#### rejects-and-reports a runtime-computed false through expect_not

- Verify: expect_not accepts a runtime-computed false


<details>
<summary>Executable SSpec</summary>

Runnable source: 3 lines folded for reproduction.
Reproduction: this block contains the complete executable scenario source.

```simple
# @req: REQ-SSPEC-LOCAL-001
step("Verify: expect_not accepts a runtime-computed false")
expect_not("feature".ends_with("log"))
```

</details>

#### keeps non-boolean zero equality as a chained matcher

- Verify: chained equality still works on i64 zero
   - Expected: "abc".find("c") equals `2`


<details>
<summary>Executable SSpec</summary>

Runnable source: 3 lines folded for reproduction.
Reproduction: this block contains the complete executable scenario source.

```simple
# @req: REQ-SSPEC-LOCAL-001
step("Verify: chained equality still works on i64 zero")
expect("abc".find("c")).to_equal(2)
```

</details>

